# Label memfds with the context of the process that created them, class
# memfd_file, rather than leaving them at the filesystem default of tmpfs:file.
#
# This is the gate on selinux_inode_init_security_anon() in the kernel
# (security/selinux/hooks.c): with the capability off the hook returns early
# without touching the inode, so every memfd falls back to u:object_r:tmpfs:s0
# and the memfd_file rules recovered from the stock policy never apply.
#
# It matters because memfds cross process boundaries as binder fds.
# security_binder_transfer_file() checks the receiving domain against the
# inode's own label, so an unlabelled memfd makes binder_translate_fd() return
# -EPERM, the reply fails with FAILED_TRANSACTION, and the sender sees a
# transaction error with no obvious cause. The camera HAL dies that way: the
# GXP/Tachyon session reply carries a MessageQueue memfd, the fd cannot be
# installed, "Unable to create dsp context" follows, and a CHECK aborts the
# provider, leaving zero camera devices.
#
# The obvious fix, granting tmpfs:file to the receiving domains, is wrong. It
# would open every tmpfs file to them rather than the one fd being passed, and
# it diverges from the stock policy this device's HALs were written against.
policycap memfd_class;
